    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.7, 2.6.23 (Pages 83, 84, 85, 86, 93, 94)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>An impressive water system was discovered within the fortifications of Tel Gezer, comprising an entrance, a sloping tunnel with steps, a collection basin, and a cave, dating to the Middle Bronze Age. A large, complex stepped water tunnel, 40m long and nearly 40m deep, leading to a natural spring/groundwater source. The author argues for its construction in the Middle Bronze Age. Numerous bottle-shaped, rock-cut, and often plastered cisterns were found, typically 4.9-7m deep, with drainage channels collecting runoff. Specific examples like 23089 and 34046 are dated to the Middle Bronze Age, with some reused into later periods.</p>]]></description>
