    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Upper Galilee</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.8, 2.6.21 (Pages 87, 91, 92)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A large depression in the lower city is suggested to be the top of a shaft dug to groundwater, used during the Middle Bronze and Late Bronze Ages. Six bottle-shaped, rock-cut cisterns (e.g., 9024, 9027, 6243) were found, dated to MB II by pottery, with some continuing into LB. They featured narrow necks, expanding bodies, and sometimes plaster, designed to collect rainwater runoff.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Middle_Bronze</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Hazor</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל חצור</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Upper Galilee</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-2200 to -1550 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.6.8, 2.6.21</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>87, 91, 92</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>A large depression in the lower city is suggested to be the top of a shaft dug to groundwater, used during the Middle Bronze and Late Bronze Ages. Six bottle-shaped, rock-cut cisterns (e.g., 9024, 9027, 6243) were found, dated to MB II by pottery, with some continuing into LB. They featured narrow necks, expanding bodies, and sometimes plaster, designed to collect rainwater runoff.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.56635,33.01685,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Kabri (תל כברי)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Kabri (תל כברי)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Western Galilee</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.1 (Pages 73)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A large planned city from MB II (c. 1800 BCE) was built around the abundant Ein Shefa spring, which was enclosed within the city&apos;s rampart. Excess water was likely drained through a channel in the western gate.</p>]]></description>
